AD  - Department of Civil and Environmental Engineering, University of Alberta, Edmonton, Alta. T6G 2M8, Canada
AB  - A field study was undertaken in a rural community (High Level, Alberta) using low volume air sampling coupled with elemental analysis to characterize source contributions of PM10 to community ambient air. The community has a wood waste burner that was believed to be contributing to local particulate matter air pollution. A MiniVol low volume air sampler was colocated with a reference method sampler (TEOM-Series 1400a continuous ambient particulate monitor) for measurement of PM10 at three community locations. A portable meteorological station was also placed at sampling locations in order to obtain meteorological data. Randomly selected individual PM10 particles collected on filter samples were analyzed by Scanning Electron Microscopy-Energy Dispersive X-ray (SEM-EDX) analysis. Receptor modeling of elemental data utilizing principle component analysis (PCA) apportioned ambient PM10 among six probable sources that accounted for 79% of total variance of the original data set. These included crustal material (26%), road dust (13%), road salts (12%), vehicles (11%), residual oil (10%), and sulfates (7%). Based on evaluation of the field data, re-suspension sources of particulate matter and local meteorology were the two most important factors contributing to PM10 in community ambient air. Wood waste burner operation did not have a quantifiable role in influencing airborne particulate matter concentrations during sampling at the three locations monitored in High Level.
