TI  - On the MicroPEM as an ambient particulate matter sampler
PY  - 2016
T2  - Air and Waste Management Association - Air Quality Measurement Methods and Technology Conference 2016
SP  - 89
EP  - 95
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/84981275885?origin=resultslist
AD  - RTI International, Research Triangle Park, NC, United States
AB  - The MicroPEM has potential to be an ambient air quality sensor that provides accurate and precise, spatially-temporally resolved PM2.5 concentration data when used appropriately. Users must understand the limitations of the MicroPEM, as well as other low-cost sensors, when used in ambient environments if PM concentration data quality is to be maximized. The most important aspect is the application of a correction factor to account for bias in the MicroPEM nephelometer concentration. Uncorrected nephelometer concentration data affects the slope and intercept of a regression analysis against a FEM. A correction factor is easily obtained by performing gravimetric analysis on all or a subset of the MicroPEM filters. If a network of MicroPEMs is to be deployed, the high precision between MicroPEMs enables a FEM collocated with a MicroPEM at one location to provide the correction factor for the entire MicroPEM network. A final consideration is understanding the accuracy of the MicroPEM, in its current format, at PM concentrations lower than 30μg/m3 is affected by the 3μg/m3 bin size. This will result in an increase in the bias and a decrease in the coefficient of determination. New MicroPEM firmware will be available in late 2016 to minimize this effect.
