AB  - Air quality monitoring in Southeast Ohio was conducted and data from the two sites (Lebanon and Sycamore) were analyzed. The area is known to have multiple particulate matter (PM) sources, e.g., various industries, transportation, residences, etc. and complex weather conditions, which can all contribute to difference in monitoring results. For Sycamore, the regression of the whole year's data showed good agreement in a linear regression model, and good agreement was also obtained in some months. Different continuous instruments showed different results. This was expected as these instruments were based on different operating principles, and also the complexity of the PM sources and ambient conditions. Continuous sample collection occurred in diurnal ambient temperature also with different humidity. During winter, continuous method showed differences up 17% higher than the Federal Reference Method. This is an abstract of a paper presented at the 104th AWMA Annual Conference and Exhibition 2011 (Orlando, FL 6/21-24/2011).
